/* V49 — dread belongs in the bed, definition belongs in the hit.

   The note was that the sounds should be mysterious, frightening and tense, and to go and find out
   what actually does that. So I did, and the reading changes two things: it adds four techniques
   the game did not have, and it says one thing I did in V47 was aimed at the wrong layer.

   ---- What the literature actually says ----

   **Close-interval dissonance.** Two tones about a semitone apart do not beat pleasantly, they
   *clash*: the frequencies fall inside one critical band and the result is rough and refuses to
   resolve. This is the most reliably unsettling interval there is, and it is the one V35
   deliberately rejected — "a minor second in the bass is pure fright", which was correct when the
   brief was tension with only a trace of fright. The brief is now explicitly frightening, so it
   comes back.

   **Low, unsteady sound.** The infrasound result is real but is routinely overstated, and for this
   game it is unusable as stated: the documented stress response sits around 18–19Hz, and no phone
   speaker reproduces 18Hz at any volume. Pretending otherwise would be writing a tone nobody can
   hear. What survives a phone speaker is a low bed that is *unstable* — so the sub is amplitude
   modulated a few times a second, which is audible on any speaker and reads as something wrong
   with the floor rather than as a note.

   **The Shepard–Risset glissando.** A stack of octave-spaced voices, each gliding upward, each
   fading in at the bottom of its sweep and out at the top, so the pitch appears to rise forever
   while the mix never gets louder. This is the technique for mounting pressure that does not
   consume headroom, recommended at roughly 0.1–0.2Hz. It is a near-perfect fit for a wave arena:
   pressure is exactly what this game has and exactly what it was not expressing.

   **Dynamic range, and silence to sit in.** Start quiet so that loud lands. V35 already built the
   hush, which is the hard part; what it lacked was anything sustained for the hush to be a hole in.

   ---- And what it says about V47 ----

   V47 removed the noise from the kill, which was right — the drum complaint was accurate. But it
   also gave the kill an eighteen-millisecond attack and no transient at all, and that overshot. The
   game-feel literature is consistent that player-action feedback has to *confirm*: a hit sound
   carries information, and a hit with no edge on the front of it reads as mushy rather than as
   tense. Tension and confirmation are not in conflict, because they are not the same layer.

   So the division of labour is now explicit, which is what every adaptive-audio guide describes:
   **the bed carries the dread, the hit carries the information.** The kill gets an edge back — a
   pitched ping, three milliseconds, not noise, so it does not become a drum again — and everything
   frightening moves into a sustained layer underneath where it can be continuous, which is the one
   thing a per-kill sound can never be. */

const V49={
 /* One semitone. The interval itself is the effect. */
 minor2:Math.pow(2,1/12),
 /* Slow enough to be heard as unsteadiness rather than as a tremolo effect. */
 wobble:4.6,
 /* Octaves the Risset spans, and how long one octave of apparent rise takes. 7s is inside the
    0.1–0.2Hz the literature recommends. */
 rissetVoices:4,
 rissetPeriod:7,
 /* The bed is meant to be felt and not noticed. Anything above about .1 and it stops being a floor
    and starts being a part. */
 bedGain:.085,
 rissetGain:.05
};

/* ---- The bed --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Built once and held, rather than scheduled per event. That is the whole point: a one-shot cue is
   a scare, and a scare is not tension. Tension is the thing that was already there when you
   started paying attention.

   Routed through `audio.music` rather than `audio.sfxGain` so it obeys the music slider and so
   V35's dread filter closes over it — when things go badly the bed loses its top end along with
   the arrangement, which is free and is exactly right. */
audio.v49=null;

audio.bedStart=function(){
 if(!this.ctx||this.v49)return;
 const t=this.ctx.currentTime;
 const root=typeof v34Root==='function'?v34Root():55;
 const out=this.ctx.createGain();
 out.gain.value=.0001;
 const filter=this.ctx.createBiquadFilter();
 filter.type='lowpass';
 filter.frequency.value=700;
 filter.Q.value=.7;
 filter.connect(out);
 out.connect(this.music);

 const nodes=[];

 /* 1. The floor. A clean sub, then an LFO on its own gain so it never sits still. This is the
       honest substitute for the infrasound result — same intent, at a frequency that exists on the
       device the game is played on. */
 const sub=this.ctx.createOscillator();
 const subGain=this.ctx.createGain();
 sub.type='sine';
 sub.frequency.value=root;
 subGain.gain.value=.0001;
 const lfo=this.ctx.createOscillator();
 const lfoDepth=this.ctx.createGain();
 lfo.type='sine';
 lfo.frequency.value=V49.wobble;
 lfoDepth.gain.value=0;
 lfo.connect(lfoDepth);
 lfoDepth.connect(subGain.gain);
 sub.connect(subGain);
 subGain.connect(filter);
 sub.start(t);lfo.start(t);
 nodes.push(sub,lfo);

 /* 2. The clash. Two voices a semitone apart, each detuned a few cents off its partner so the pair
       also drifts slowly — the semitone supplies the roughness, the detune supplies the
       unsteadiness, and neither on its own does both. Two octaves up from the sub, because in the
       bass a minor second is just mud on a small speaker. */
 const clash=this.ctx.createGain();
 clash.gain.value=.0001;
 clash.connect(filter);
 for(const [mult,cents] of [[4,-7],[4*V49.minor2,6]]){
  const osc=this.ctx.createOscillator();
  osc.type='triangle';
  osc.frequency.value=root*mult;
  osc.detune.value=cents;
  osc.connect(clash);
  osc.start(t);
  nodes.push(osc);
 }

 /* 3. The rise. Octave-spaced sines whose frequency and level are set from one shared phase every
       update — a real endless glissando rather than a sample of one, so it can be held for as long
       as the pressure lasts and can stop the moment it does not. */
 const risset=this.ctx.createGain();
 risset.gain.value=.0001;
 risset.connect(filter);
 const voices=[];
 for(let i=0;i<V49.rissetVoices;i++){
  const osc=this.ctx.createOscillator();
  const g=this.ctx.createGain();
  osc.type='sine';
  osc.frequency.value=root*Math.pow(2,i);
  g.gain.value=.0001;
  osc.connect(g);g.connect(risset);
  osc.start(t);
  voices.push({osc,g});
  nodes.push(osc);
 }

 this.v49={out,filter,subGain,lfoDepth,clash,risset,voices,nodes,root,phase:0,at:0};
};

audio.bedStop=function(){
 const bed=this.v49;
 if(!bed||!this.ctx)return;
 this.v49=null;
 const t=this.ctx.currentTime;
 /* Faded rather than cut. A drone that stops instantly is a glitch, and the release is also the
    last half-second of a run, which is not a moment to put a click in. */
 bed.out.gain.cancelScheduledValues(t);
 bed.out.gain.setTargetAtTime(.0001,t,.25);
 for(const node of bed.nodes){try{node.stop(t+1.2);}catch(e){/* already stopped */}}
 setTimeout(()=>{try{bed.out.disconnect();}catch(e){/* context gone */}},1600);
};

/* Pressure is not dread. V35's dread is about what you have left to lose and it decays slowly;
   pressure is about what is on the field right now, so it climbs through a wave and falls the
   moment the field is cleared. The rise tracks pressure because an endless glissando that never
   resolves is unbearable — it has to be allowed to stop, and a cleared field is what earns that. */
function v49Pressure(){
 if(!game.running||game.paused)return 0;
 const line=H-(isMobile?105:72);
 let closest=0;
 let live=0;
 for(const o of game.orbs){
  if(o.y<0)continue;
  live++;
  closest=Math.max(closest,Math.min(1,o.y/line));
 }
 return Math.min(1,
  Math.min(1,live/9)*.45
  +Math.pow(closest,2)*.4
  +(game.boss?.35:0));
}

/* Parameters are driven at 12Hz rather than per frame. Every one of these is either a filter sweep
   or a slow glissando — none of them can change meaningfully inside 80ms — and the update runs
   inside the frame loop of a game whose speed is a standing complaint. */
const V49_TICK=1/12;

const v49AudioUpdate=audio.update.bind(audio);
audio.update=function(g){
 v49AudioUpdate(g);
 const bed=this.v49;
 if(!bed||!this.ctx)return;
 const now=this.ctx.currentTime;
 if(now-bed.at<V49_TICK)return;
 const step=Math.min(.5,now-bed.at||V49_TICK);
 bed.at=now;

 const dread=Math.max(0,Math.min(1,g.v35Dread||0));
 const pressure=v49Pressure();
 const live=g.running&&!g.paused;
 const tc=.22;

 /* The bed ducks out of the hush instead of playing through it. V35 bought a beat of nothing and
    the whole value of it is that it is nothing — a drone running underneath would spend it. */
 const open=live&&!(g.v35Hush>0)?1:0;
 bed.out.gain.setTargetAtTime(Math.max(.0001,V49.bedGain*open),now,tc);

 /* The floor is always there; how unsteady it is tracks dread. At zero it is a held note, and by
    the last life it is visibly breathing. */
 bed.subGain.gain.setTargetAtTime(.5+dread*.22,now,tc);
 bed.lfoDepth.gain.setTargetAtTime(.1+dread*.42,now,tc);

 /* The clash is the frightening element, so it is the one that is rationed. Near-silent while a
    run is going well, and it only really arrives when the player is in trouble — which is what
    makes it mean something when it does. */
 bed.clash.gain.setTargetAtTime(Math.max(.0001,.05+Math.pow(dread,1.6)*.85),now,tc);
 bed.filter.frequency.setTargetAtTime(400+(1-dread)*900,now,tc);

 /* The rise runs on pressure. Measured over a real run its level moves across a threefold range
    and does reach zero, but a wave arena almost always has something on the field, so in practice
    this swells and subsides rather than switching off — the thing that actually silences it is the
    hush, which ducks the whole bed. */
 bed.risset.gain.setTargetAtTime(Math.max(.0001,Math.pow(pressure,1.4)*V49.rissetGain/V49.bedGain),now,tc);
 if(pressure<=.02)return;

 bed.phase=(bed.phase+step/V49.rissetPeriod)%1;
 const span=V49.rissetVoices;
 for(let i=0;i<span;i++){
  /* Each voice climbs one octave per period and wraps. Because the set is octave-spaced and the
     wrap point is masked by the envelope, the ear hears one tone rising without end rather than
     four tones looping. */
  const pos=(bed.phase+i)%span;
  const voice=bed.voices[i];
  voice.osc.frequency.setTargetAtTime(bed.root*Math.pow(2,pos),now,.08);
  /* Zero at both ends of the sweep: a voice must be inaudible at the moment it jumps back to the
     bottom, or the illusion collapses into an obvious sawtooth of pitch. */
  const bell=Math.pow(Math.sin(Math.PI*(pos/span)),2);
  voice.g.gain.setTargetAtTime(Math.max(.0001,bell*.5),now,.1);
 }
};

/* ---- Lifecycle ---- */

const v49Begin=begin;
begin=function(mode='solo',seed){
 v49Begin(mode,seed);
 if(audio.ctx&&save.music>0)audio.bedStart();
};

const v49Finish=finish;
finish=function(victory=false,duelData=null){
 v49Finish(victory,duelData);
 audio.bedStop();
};

/* Leaving to a menu mid-run has to stop it too, or the drone follows the player out of the arena
   and plays under the launch screen. */
const v49ShowScreen=showScreen;
showScreen=function(id){
 if(id!=='gameScreen')audio.bedStop();
 return v49ShowScreen(id);
};

/* ---- The hush gets its minor second back ----

   V35 chose a perfect fifth here over a minor second, and said why: the brief at the time was
   tension with only a trace of fright, and a minor second in the bass is pure fright. That
   reasoning was sound and the brief has since changed. The fifth stays as the body of the swell —
   it is what makes the hush read as a system spooling up rather than as a jump scare — and the
   minor second goes underneath it, quiet, as the thing that stops the swell from resolving. */
const v49Dread=audio.dread.bind(audio);
audio.dread=function(){
 v49Dread();
 if(!this.ctx||!save.sfx)return;
 const root=typeof v34Root==='function'?v34Root():55;
 const t=this.ctx.currentTime;
 const g=this.ctx.createGain();
 g.gain.setValueAtTime(.0001,t);
 g.gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(.075,t+V35.hush*.8);
 g.gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(.0001,t+V35.hush+.45);
 for(const mult of [2,2*V49.minor2]){
  const osc=this.ctx.createOscillator();
  osc.type='triangle';
  osc.frequency.setValueAtTime(root*mult,t);
  osc.connect(g);
  osc.start(t);osc.stop(t+V35.hush+.5);
 }
 g.connect(this.sfxGain);
};

/* ---- The hit gets its edge back ----

   Three milliseconds of a pitched ping on the front of the kill. It is a tone and not a noise
   burst, so the drum does not come back; what it restores is the moment of confirmation, which is
   the part of a hit sound that carries information rather than mood. The sweep's own attack comes
   down from eighteen milliseconds to nine for the same reason — eighteen was chosen to be as far
   from a click as possible, and that was further than it needed to go. */
V47.attack=.009;

audio.edge=function(freq,gain,pan){
 if(!this.ctx)return;
 const t=this.ctx.currentTime;
 const osc=this.ctx.createOscillator();
 const g=this.ctx.createGain();
 const p=this.ctx.createStereoPanner?this.ctx.createStereoPanner():null;
 osc.type='triangle';
 osc.frequency.setValueAtTime(freq*2,t);
 osc.frequency.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(Math.max(30,freq*1.4),t+.05);
 g.gain.setValueAtTime(.0001,t);
 g.gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(Math.max(.0002,gain),t+.003);
 g.gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(.0001,t+.055);
 osc.connect(g);
 if(p){p.pan.value=Math.max(-1,Math.min(1,pan));g.connect(p);p.connect(this.sfxGain);}
 else g.connect(this.sfxGain);
 osc.start(t);osc.stop(t+.08);
};

const v49Impact=audio.impact.bind(audio);
audio.impact=function(type,power=1,combo=0,perfect=false){
 if(!this.ctx)return;
 /* Read before the base call, because the base call is what advances the window counter — and a
    kill that the budget is about to drop should not get an edge either. */
 const dropped=(this.ctx.currentTime-v34LastAt<.045)&&v34InWindow>=3;
 v49Impact(type,power,combo,perfect);
 if(dropped)return;
 const kit=V34_KITS[type]||V34_KITS.normal;
 this.edge(v34Note(kit,combo),Math.min(.34,kit.gain*power*.3),(Math.random()-.5)*.8);
};
